C = [
("h1", "1. Malachi’s Close: The Law, Horeb and the Curse"),
("p", "Claim: the promise of Elijah is the last paragraph of the Old Testament, and it cannot be read apart from the paragraph around it. Malachi 4:4–6 joins three things: a command to remember the law of Moses given at Horeb, a promise to send Elijah the prophet, and a warning of a curse. The prophet who is promised is promised to Israel, before a day, and for a work done on hearts. The volume reads the three verses in order and asks what each says and what each leaves unsaid."),
("sub", "1.1 The book and its audience"),
("p", "The book opens, “The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi” (Malachi 1:1). The audience is named in the first verse and is never changed. The book that follows is a series of disputes between the LORD and Israel in which the people ask, “Wherein hast thou loved us?” (Malachi 1:2), “Wherein have we despised thy name?” (Malachi 1:6) and “Wherein have we wearied him?” (Malachi 2:17). The priests are charged with offering polluted bread, the people with unfaithfulness to the wife of their youth, and all with robbing God in tithes and offerings (Malachi 1:6–8; 2:14–16; 3:8). The book is the LORD’s indictment of his people and a promise to them at the same time. The second volume of this series reads the charges against the priests and the offerings; this chapter needs only the point that the audience of the last paragraph is the audience of the first."),
("sub", "1.2 The day that burns"),
("p", "The last paragraph begins in the verses before it. Malachi 3:1–5 announced a messenger, the sudden coming of the LORD to his temple, and a judgment on sorcerers, adulterers and false swearers. Malachi 3:13–18 records the faithful speaking to one another, a book of remembrance, and the promise, “And they shall be mine, saith the LORD of hosts, in that day when I make up my jewels” (Malachi 3:17). Then chapter 4 opens with the day itself: “For, behold, the day cometh, that shall burn as an oven; and all the proud, yea, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble: and the day that cometh shall burn them up, saith the LORD of hosts, that it shall leave them neither root nor branch” (Malachi 4:1)."),
("p", "Two outcomes are set side by side. The proud and the wicked are stubble. “But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall” (Malachi 4:2). The speaker turns from the day in the third person to a “you” who fear his name, and the “you” is Israel in the book’s own address. “And ye shall tread down the wicked; for they shall be ashes under the soles of your feet in the day that I shall do this, saith the LORD of hosts” (Malachi 4:3). The day is a day of fire and a day of healing, and the same day does both. It is the day the next three verses are about."),
("sub", "1.3 Remember the law of Moses"),
("p", "Between the day and the prophet stands a command: “Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel, with the statutes and judgments” (Malachi 4:4). The sentence is easily passed over, and it should not be. It names the law as Moses’ and as the LORD’s, it names Horeb as the place it was given, and it names “all Israel” as the people it was given to. Malachi is the last of the prophets in the English Old Testament, and his last act is to point back to the first foundation of Israel’s life with God. The book ends looking at Sinai and looking forward to a day."),
("p", "The order of the three verses matters. The command to remember the law is not a charge against a particular sin. It is a call to the whole people to hold what was given. It stands before the promise of the prophet, and nothing in the paragraph says why the command stands there. The volume notes the placement and builds nothing on it, except one thing: the promise that follows is a promise in Israel’s line, to a people with a law, a land and a temple, and not a promise addressed to anyone outside that line."),
("sub", "1.4 Elijah the prophet"),
("p", "“Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD” (Malachi 4:5). Eight things can be read out of the verse without adding anything. The speaker is the LORD: “I will send.” The sending is future. It is addressed to a plural “you,” the same Israel of the book. The one sent is named, “Elijah,” and given a title, “the prophet.” The title is the one the book of Kings gives him when he comes near the altar on Carmel: “Elijah the prophet came near, and said, LORD God of Abraham, Isaac, and of Israel” (1 Kings 18:36). The sending is set before an event, “the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.” The day is called great and dreadful, and the verse does not say what happens in it. And the verse says nothing of his baptizing, preaching in a wilderness or announcing a person. Those come from other texts."),
("p", "The volume draws one conclusion at this point and no more. The verse names a man. A reader may say that the name is a title for an office or a type, and that is the position of some writers who are answered in the last chapter. The verse as written says “Elijah the prophet,” and the same name and title are used in the narrative of Kings of a man who lived and spoke. Nothing in the verse itself marks the name as figurative. The volume therefore reads the promise as a promise of Elijah, and tests that reading against every passage that follows."),
("sub", "1.5 What the prophet does, and the curse"),
("p", "“And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse” (Malachi 4:6). The work is a work on hearts. The “fathers” and the “children” are not further identified by Malachi, and the volume does not guess. The purpose clause, “lest I come and smite the earth with a curse,” gives the work its reason. If the hearts are not turned, the LORD comes and smites. The last word of the Old Testament in the English Bible is “curse,” and the word is the sign of the Mosaic covenant’s own sanctions: Deuteronomy 28 sets blessings and curses before Israel (Deuteronomy 28:15). The promise of Elijah is a promise of a work meant to avert the curse."),
("p", "Two things follow, and the later chapters will test them. The first is that the work is conditional in its end. The prophet turns hearts so that the LORD does not have to smite, and the verse states no guarantee that all the hearts are turned. The second is that the work comes before the day and is aimed at it. The day is not the prophet’s coming; the prophet’s coming is a mercy before the day."),
("ctbl", "Malachi 4:4–6 in four questions.", [
    ("Question", "The text", "Passage"),
    ("Who speaks?", "The LORD of hosts, to Israel by Malachi", "Malachi 1:1; 4:3"),
    ("Who is sent?", "Elijah the prophet, by name and by title", "Malachi 4:5; 1 Kings 18:36"),
    ("When?", "Before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD", "Malachi 4:5"),
    ("To do what?", "To turn the heart of the fathers to the children and the children to their fathers, lest the LORD come and smite the earth with a curse", "Malachi 4:6"),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "1.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Malachi is addressed to Israel from its first verse to its last, and the promise of Elijah is a promise to that people.",
    "The command to remember the law of Moses given at Horeb stands between the day and the prophet, and the promise belongs to Israel’s line.",
    "Malachi 4:5 names a man, Elijah the prophet, and gives him the title the book of Kings gives him; nothing in the verse marks the name as a figure.",
    "The prophet’s work is on hearts and has a purpose, that the LORD not come and smite the earth with a curse.",
    "The promise is a promise of a mercy before the day, and the day is not the prophet’s coming.",
  ]),
]
